#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:2
msgid "Standard task options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:5
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:7
msgid ""
"The following options can be configured on your tasks and are not specific "
"to any particular task type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:9
msgid "**help** : ``str`` :doc:`📖<../guides/help_guide>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:8 ../../../tasks/options.rst:20
#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:34
msgid "str 📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:10
msgid ""
"Help text to be displayed next to the task name in the documentation when "
"poe is run without specifying a task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:12
msgid ""
"**args** : ``dict[str, dict]`` | ``list[Union[str, dict]]`` :doc:`📖<../"
"guides/args_guide>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:11
msgid "dict[str, dict] | list[Union[str, dict]] 📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:13
msgid ""
"Define CLI options, positional arguments, or flags that this task should "
"accept."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:15
msgid ""
"**env** :  ``dict[str, str]`` :ref:`📖<Setting task specific environment "
"variables>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:14 ../../../tasks/options.rst:27
msgid "dict[str, str] 📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:16
msgid "A map of environment variables to be set for this task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:18
msgid ""
"**envfile** :  ``str`` | ``list[str]`` :ref:`📖<Loading environment "
"variables from an env file>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:17 ../../../tasks/options.rst:31
msgid "str | list[str] 📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:19
msgid "Provide one or more env files to be loaded before running this task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:21
msgid ""
"**cwd** :  ``str`` :ref:`📖<Running a task with a specific working "
"directory>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:22
msgid ""
"Specify the current working directory that this task should run with. The "
"given path is resolved relative to the parent directory of the ``pyproject."
"toml``, or it may be absolute. Resolves environment variables in the format "
"``${VAR_NAME}``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:25
msgid "**deps** :  ``list[str]`` :doc:`📖<../guides/composition_guide>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:24
msgid "list[str] 📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:26
msgid "A list of task invocations that will be executed before this one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:28
msgid "**uses** :  ``dict[str, str]`` :doc:`📖<../guides/composition_guide>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:29
msgid ""
"Allows this task to use the output of other tasks which are executed first. "
"The value is a map where the values are invocations of the other tasks, and "
"the keys are environment variables by which the results of those tasks will "
"be accessible in this task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:32
msgid ""
"**uses_env** :  ``str`` | ``list[str]`` :doc:`📖<../guides/"
"composition_guide>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:33
msgid ""
"Allows this task to use the output of other tasks which are executed first, "
"and their output is parsed like an env file to get zero or more environment "
"variables which will be accessible in this task. The referenced task "
"determines which variables (if any) are set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:35
msgid "**capture_stdout** : ``str`` :ref:`📖<Redirect task output to a file>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:36
msgid "Causes the task output to be redirected to a file with the given path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:38
msgid ""
"**executor** : ``str`` | ``dict[str, str]`` :ref:`📖<Configure the executor "
"for a task>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:37
msgid "str | dict[str, str] 📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:39
msgid "Specify executor type and/or configuration for this task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:41
msgid "**verbosity** : ``int`` :ref:`📖<Configure task level verbosity>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:40
msgid "int 📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:42
msgid ""
"Specify the verbosity level for this task, from -2 (least verbose) to 2 "
"(most verbose), overriding the project level verbosity setting, which "
"defaults to 0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:44
msgid ""
"**use_exec** : ``bool`` :ref:`📖<Defining tasks that run via exec instead of "
"a subprocess>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:43
msgid "bool 📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:45
msgid ""
"Specify that this task should be executed in the same process, instead of as "
"a subprocess."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:49
msgid ""
"This option is only applicable to **cmd**, **script**, and **expr** tasks, "
"and it implies the task in question cannot be referenced by another task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:52
msgid "Setting task specific environment variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:54
msgid ""
"You can specify arbitrary environment variables to be set for a single task "
"by providing the env option like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:56
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"script = \"myapp:run\"\n"
"env = { PORT = \"9001\" }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:62
msgid ""
"Notice this example uses deep keys which can be more convenient but aren't "
"as well supported by some older toml implementations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:64
msgid ""
"If the variable name starts with ``_`` and contains no uppercase characters "
"then it will be private and not actually exposed as an environment variable "
"on the task subprocess, however it will be usable in task configuration, or "
"cmd task parameter expansion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:66
msgid ""
"For example private variables can be inherited by subtasks and then aliased "
"to standard variables by interpolating them into the variable definition "
"like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:68
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.run]\n"
"ref = \"_serve\"\n"
"args = [\"_favorite_number\"]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks._serve]\n"
"help = \"This indirection is pointless but illustrates the point\"\n"
"script = \"myapp:run\"\n"
"env = { PORT = \"${_favorite_number}\" }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:81
msgid "Setting defaults for environment variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:83
msgid ""
"The previous example can be modified to only set the `PORT` variable if it "
"is not already set by replacing the last line with the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:85
msgid "env.PORT.default = \"9001\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:91
msgid "Templating environment variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:93
msgid ""
"It is also possible to reference existing environment variables when "
"defining a new environment variable for a task, including using the :ref:"
"`default and alternate value operators <parameter-expansion-operators>`. "
"This may be useful for aliasing or extending a variable already defined in "
"the host environment, globally in the config, or in a referenced envfile. In "
"the following example the value from $TF_VAR_service_port on the host "
"environment is also made available as $FLASK_RUN_PORT within the task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:95
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"cmd = \"flask run\"\n"
"env = { FLASK_RUN_PORT = \"${TF_VAR_service_port}\" }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:105
msgid "Loading environment variables from an env file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:107
msgid ""
"You can also specify one or more env files (with bash-like syntax) to load "
"per task like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:109
msgid ""
"# .env\n"
"STAGE=dev\n"
"PASSWORD='!@#$%^&*('"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:115
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks]\n"
"serve.script  = \"myapp:run\"\n"
"serve.envfile = \".env\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:121
msgid ""
"The envfile option accepts the name (or relative path) to a single envfile "
"as shown above but can also by given a list of such paths like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:123
msgid "serve.envfile = [\".env\", \"local.env\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:127
msgid ""
"Normally a missing envfile results in a warning, however optional envfiles "
"can be indicated with the following structure including the ``optional`` "
"key, in contrast with the ``expected`` key:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:129
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve.envfile]\n"
"optional = [\"local.env\"]\n"
"expected = [\"base.env\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:135
msgid ""
"Files are loaded in the listed order, optional files are loaded after "
"expected files. Last file wins in case of conflicts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:137
msgid ""
"Normally envfile paths are resolved relative to the project root (that is "
"the parent directory of the pyproject.toml). However when working with a "
"monorepo it can also be useful to specify the path relative to the root of "
"the git repository, which can be done by referencing the ``POE_GIT_DIR`` or "
"``POE_GIT_ROOT`` variables like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:139
msgid ""
"[tool.poe]\n"
"envfile = \"${POE_GIT_DIR}/.env\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:144
msgid ""
"See the documentation on :ref:`Special variables<Special variables>` for a "
"full explanation of how these variables work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:146
msgid ""
"Env files support parameter expansion: ``$VAR``, ``${VAR}``, ``${VAR:-"
"default}``, and ``${VAR:+alternate}`` are expanded in unquoted and double-"
"quoted values. Each variable can reference variables defined earlier in the "
"same file, as well as variables already set in the environment (host env "
"vars, global env, or parent task env vars). Single-quoted values are never "
"expanded."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:150
msgid ""
"For a more detailed explanation see the documentation for :ref:`the envfile "
"global option<Loading external environment variables>` which works in the "
"same way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:154
msgid "Running a task with a specific working directory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:156
msgid ""
"By default tasks are run from the project root – that is the parent "
"directory of the pyproject.toml file. However if a task needs to be run in "
"another directory then this can be accomplished by using the :toml:`cwd` "
"option like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:158
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.build-client]\n"
"cmd = \"npx ts-node -T ./build.ts\"\n"
"cwd = \"./client\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:164
msgid ""
"In this example, the npx executable is executed inside the :sh:`./client` "
"subdirectory of the project (when ``cwd`` is a relative path, it gets "
"resolved relatively to the project root), and will use the nodejs package."
"json configuration from that location and evaluate paths relative to that "
"location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:166
msgid ""
"The ``cwd`` option also accepts absolute paths and resolves environment "
"variables in the format ``${VAR_NAME}``, including the :ref:`default and "
"alternate value operators <parameter-expansion-operators>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:168
msgid ""
"Poe provides its own :sh:`$POE_PWD` variable that is by default set to the "
"directory, from which poe was executed; this may be overridden by setting "
"the variable to a different value beforehand. Using :sh:`$POE_PWD`, a task's "
"working directory may be set to the one from which it was executed like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:170
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.convert]\n"
"script = \"my_project.conversion_tool:main\"\n"
"cwd = \"${POE_PWD}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:178
msgid "Redirect task output to a file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:180
msgid ""
"You can configure poe to redirect the standard output of a task to a file on "
"disk by providing the ``capture_stdout`` option like so."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:182
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"cmd            = \"gunicorn ./my_app:run\"\n"
"capture_stdout = \"gunicorn_log.txt\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:188
msgid ""
"If a relative path is provided, as in the example above, then it will be "
"resolved relative to the project root directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:190
msgid ""
"The ``capture_stdout`` option supports referencing environment variables, "
"including the :ref:`default and alternate value operators <parameter-"
"expansion-operators>`. For example setting ``capture_stdout = \"${POE_PWD}/"
"output.txt\"`` will cause the output file to be created within the current "
"working directory of the parent process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:194
msgid ""
"The ``capture_stdout`` is incompatible with the ``use_exec`` option, and "
"tasks that declare it cannot be referenced by another task via the ``uses`` "
"option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:196
msgid ""
"The value ``/dev/null`` or ``NUL`` may be used to discard all output from "
"the task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:200
msgid "Configure the executor for a task"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:202
msgid ""
"You can specify a different executor for a task by providing the "
"``executor`` option like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:204
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"cmd      = \"gunicorn ./my_app:run\"\n"
"executor = { type = \"virtualenv\", location = \"./server.venv\" }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:210
msgid ""
"This works exactly like the the global option to :ref:`configure the "
"executor<Configure the executor>` except it only impacts the one task. If "
"the task does not specify a different executor type, then it will inherit "
"from and extend the global executor configuration (assuming the type is the "
"same)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:212
msgid ""
"If you only want to change the executor type but not provide any additional "
"configuration, you can also specify the executor as a simple string like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:214
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"cmd      = \"gunicorn ./my_app:run\"\n"
"executor = \"poetry\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:222
msgid ""
"You can also configure the executor at the :ref:`project level<Configure the "
"executor>` or at the :ref:`task group level<Grouping tasks>`, which will "
"have lower precedence. Alternatively you can override the executor type at "
"runtime by passing the ``--executor`` CLI option (before the task name) with "
"the name of the executor to use, and pass executor options via the ``--"
"executor-opt`` global CLI option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:227
msgid "Configure task level virtualenv with uv"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:229
msgid ""
"The uv executor supports configuring uv to run a task with its own "
"environment. This is a powerful feature that allows Poe the Poet + uv to be "
"used as a :ref:`lightweight alternative to tools like tox<Replacing tox with "
"uv and Poe the Poet>` for testing against multiple python versions or "
"dependency sets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:231
msgid ""
"[tool.poe]\n"
"executor = \"uv\"\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.test-py311]\n"
"help     = \"An alias for the test task that runs with python 3.11\"\n"
"cmd      = \"pytest\"\n"
"executor = {isolated = true, python = \"3.11\"}\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.test-py312]\n"
"help     = \"An alias for the test task that runs with python 3.12\"\n"
"cmd      = \"pytest\"\n"
"executor = {isolated = true, python = \"3.12\"}\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.test-matrix]\n"
"help     = \"Run tests for all python versions\"\n"
"sequence = [\"test-py311\", \"test-py312\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:250
msgid ""
"Note that it is not necessary to specify ``type = \"uv\"`` in the executor "
"configuration if the project is already configured to use the uv executor by "
"default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:252
msgid ""
"You can also specify a dependency on the task level without needing to add "
"it as a project dependency, using the ``with`` option like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:254
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.test]\n"
"help     = \"Run the tests\"\n"
"cmd      = \"pytest\"\n"
"executor = {type = \"uv\", with = [\"pytest\"], isolated = true}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:261
msgid ""
"Executor options can also be set at runtime via the ``--executor-opt`` CLI "
"option (before the task name) to override or add to the executor "
"configuration for a specific task invocation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:263
msgid ""
"poe --executor uv --executor-opt with=pytest --executor-opt isolated --"
"executor-opt with=pytest-cov --executor-opt python=3.12 test --cov my_package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:267
msgid ""
"Multiple values can be provided for options that accept lists by passing the "
"``--executor-opt`` option multiple times as shown above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:271
msgid "Configure task level verbosity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:273
msgid ""
"You can specify the verbosity level for a task by providing the :toml:"
"`verbosity` option like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:275
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.credentials]\n"
"cmd       = \"aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id creds --query "
"'SecretString'\"\n"
"verbosity = -1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:281
msgid ""
"This overrides the project level verbosity setting, which defaults to 0. The "
"verbosity level can be set to an integer from -2 (least verbose) to 2 (most "
"verbose)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:283
msgid ""
"Passing the ``-v`` or ``-q`` global options (before the task name on the "
"command line) will override increment or decrement all verbosity levels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:287
msgid "Verbosity levels"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:289
msgid ""
"The verbosity level is an integer, where positive values increase the "
"verbosity of the output, and negative values decrease it. The levels are as "
"follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:291
msgid "-3 : suppress all output, including errors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:292
msgid "-2 : suppress warning messages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:293
msgid ""
"-1 : suppress info messages such as those describing which tasks are being "
"run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:294
msgid "0 : standard verbosity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:295
msgid "1 : some extra details in output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:296
msgid "2 : more extra details in output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:297
msgid ""
"3 : debug output referencing poethepoet internals (similar to setting "
"``POE_DEBUG=1``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:299
msgid ""
"Note that the verbosity level only applies to output from poethepoet itself, "
"and does not impact the output of the tasks being run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:301
msgid ""
"Inline tasks (such as those defined within a sequence task) inherit the "
"verbosity level of their parent task, unless they explicitly override it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:303
msgid ""
"The verbosity modifying global cli options may be provided multiple times to "
"increment or decrement the verbosity level by 1 for each occurrence. For "
"example, running ``poe -qq test`` will run the ``test`` task with a "
"verbosity level of -2 relative to the baseline otherwise specified for the "
"project or task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:306
msgid "Defining tasks that run via exec instead of a subprocess"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:308
msgid ""
"Normally tasks are executed as subprocesses of the ``poe`` executable. This "
"makes it possible for poe to run multiple tasks, for example within a "
"sequence task or task graph."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:310
msgid ""
"However in certain situations it can be desirable to define a task that is "
"instead executed within the same process via an *exec* call. :doc:"
"`task_types/cmd` and :doc:`task_types/script` tasks can be configured to "
"work this way using the :toml:`use_exec` option like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:312
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"cmd      = \"gunicorn ./my_app:run\"\n"
"use_exec = true"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:320
msgid "Note the following limitations with this feature:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:322
msgid "a task configured in this way may not be referenced by another task"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/options.rst:323
msgid ""
"this does not work on windows because of `this issue <https://bugs.python."
"org/issue19066>`_. On windows a subprocess is always created."
msgstr ""
